Z||000000||p4n1c||08-21-2001||01:27 PM||||But I need to find one first.  <P>Anyone in the Toronto area know of any for sale?  Don't care what the condition is, as long as there is no frame rust(surface rust is okay, as long as it can be sanded off and leave no holes) or damage to the frame, has a V6, is somewhat drivable so I can get it to my parents house where I'll be working on it, manual or automatic doesn't matter(if it's manual I'll have to learn quick).<P>Just on the general hunt right now, if I can't afford one by the end of fall, I'll be waiting until spring.  Just on the general lookout for a good deal.<P>Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||08-21-2001||02:12 PM||||how much do you want to spend? there is a black, i think a 86gt, in the rochester, NY swap sheet - something like 50k miles ~ $3,500 US.<P>  if you have one with a solid frame what exactly needs restoring? Are you looking for one that needs repainting? drivetrain rebuild? are you looking for a basket case to nurture back to life?||||reg||1
Z||000002||p4n1c||08-21-2001||02:16 PM||||I'm looking to spend less then $700 Canadian for a basket case looking for a new life(it's going to be a sort of father/son project).  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I just need it drivable enough to get it to my parents place.||||reg||1
Z||000003||p4n1c||08-22-2001||10:09 AM||||Have people encountered any problems with restoring one these that I should be aware of?  I know there will be something, but I'm talking about things that'll happen 99% of the time when restoring one.<P>The reason I'm looking for one really cheap, is because I want to use the money to be able to get this done right and with quality parts.<P>Thanks again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||johnmac||08-22-2001||10:17 AM||||I am in the 16th month of restoring my 86GT which I bought for $500. <P>Redid the entire car inside and out.<P>Biggest problems<P>1) Engine access <BR>2) Electrical and sender problems<P>It is a challenge but worth it.<BR><P>------------------<BR>John MacHaffie<BR>86GT "Yellowbird"<BR>12+ month project car<BR>johnmac@mpinet.net<BR>Winter Springs, Florida||||reg||12
Z||000005||p4n1c||08-22-2001||10:19 AM||||Thanks for the insight.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  So I guess my asking price range is within reason.  I'll restore either notchback or fastback.  I think it will be a fun project.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tigger||08-22-2001||11:08 AM||||You want to start with a basketcase, what are you insane?!?!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P>From experience:<BR>1.  Be sure the frame is solid.<BR>2.  Engine doesn't have any death rattles.<BR>3.  Expect the first 6 months of the project to be fighting stupid nagging problems the previous owners couldn't fix and left for you.<P>Stuff I replaced immediately:<BR>Battery, alternator, soft brake lines, front calipers, battery tray, usual maintenance stuff like air filter and oil/coolants.<P>Other stuff replaced:<BR>Starter, PCV valve, wiring harness front to back, alternator belt, temp gauge sensor, headlight motor, vacuum lines, front shocks, ball joints, and tie rod ends.<P>Restoring a basketcase can be a lot of fun, but realize it requires a lot patience.  I've had some discouraging moments at times, but now it seems it was all worth it and a good learning experience.<P>Good luck in finding a Fiero!  This place is great, wish I had known about the forum when I was restoring my '84.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||p4n1c||08-22-2001||11:33 AM||||Thanks Tigger, I think it should be a fun project, and in the end I get a car that I know I put my sweat and blood into and looks the way I want it to look.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The engine will be pulled and rebuilt or swapped.  Interior's gonna be redone, same with the exterior.  <P>My dad wants to get a '72 Corvette to restore, but I think this would be a good starting project for practice, etc.  And in the process I can learn as well and get a sports car to replace my Cutlass out of it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 08-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||08-22-2001||12:06 PM||||Tigger, I think you (and your car) are an inspiration to anyone who is considering adopting and nuturing an abused /neglected Fiero.  Overcoming problems like a hacked wiring harness would probably be too much for most of us.  BTW, from the photos, yours is one of the nicer '84's I've seen.  JMHO.<P>So, have you inspired me to adopt an old beater, and make it better than new-??  Naw, too much work.  I'm just too lazy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000009||ray b||08-22-2001||01:37 PM||||spend more on a better car like a 88gt<BR>bad motor or trans only not a total resto<BR>it is all ways more than the car is worth to do a total resto on a fiero.or get a 88 4cyl<BR>and convert to a 6 if and only if body and cockpit is in good shape.<BR>it is very easy to spend $4-5000 to restore a car that will be worth less than that when done.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000010||Master_Sushi||08-22-2001||01:54 PM||||I agree with what everyone has said here. Im restoring an 84 se right now. Expect to double the amount of time and money you orignally think it will take. Also don't expect to make money on it. I know its possable with the right car, but normally when restoring a car (especially fieros) you don't make money off it. I know it is a fun project and you learn allot from it. So in the end it is worth it. I cant wait to get my 84se finished. It would probably be crushed right now if I didnt restore it, so I pretty much saved a fiero  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P>------------------<BR>88 Fiero Coupe...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mesopotamis_1.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000011||p4n1c||08-22-2001||02:05 PM||||That's what I want to do, is to save another Fiero, so it doesn't meet a worse fate.  <P>I don't mind spending the extra money to get it restored.  Because it'll be a gradual thing.  It's just I don't want to spend a lot of money on obtaining the car to restore.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||08-22-2001||02:13 PM||||My recomendations:<P>  get the best car you can to start with. Put time and money and energy into the search phase of the project and find a great deal on a car in better than average shape for a low price - it can be done - if you are not in a hurry make it a background task for you and your friends, keep you eyes open for a good deal on a car and you will come across one in 6 months to a year.<P>  It takes just as long to restore a fiero as it does a porche or corvette, or a Tiger, or an austin healy - this is not a slam against fieros but if you are gonna spend a year of your life on a project, spend it on something special, rare, or unique. If you restore an 87 GT to original condition then what do you have when you are done? a stock fiero - you can buy one for less than it will cost you to restore one. You see these in the papers ALL THE TIME: fully restored _______, $10,000 invested, asking $5,000!!! Dont let this become your story too!<P>   so either get a fiero that is already in great shape and put a better drive train in it, do a kit car body job on it, or restore a car that is rare and unique (something other than a fiero).<P>  this is not your practice life, this is your real life and you only get one - if you think you are gonna do a hack job or it wont come out all that great cause its your first car restoration, then what would you rather have at the end, a so-so fiero, or a so-so corvette, porche, vintage MG, classic roadster...??<P>  just my $0.02||||reg||10
Z||000013||p4n1c||08-22-2001||02:22 PM||||Good points and very good advice.  But the thing is.  Porsche's, Corvette's, MG's and roadsters are actually a dime a dozen here.  I see them all the time.  It's rare that I see a Fiero at all in Toronto.  I usually catch glimpses of one in a parking lot or see one on the 401.  But every other car that goes by is a Porsche, Corvette, there are about 3 MGs near my work alone.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||08-22-2001||04:25 PM||||well then let me put it another way: some cars are collectable - you can buy one and fix it up, and sell it for more than you invested in it, and if you keep if for several years its value will go up.<P> a fiero is not considered a collectors car - if you buy one for $1000 and put $4000 into it, bringing it to near showroom condition, you will be hard pressed to sell it for $3500 a year from now, and most likely it will only go down in value over time.<P> Fieros are fun cars to own and drive - I love my '85SE V6 auto, and I put enough work into it to keep it running great and looking nice - but if I were gonna spend a lot of time on a restoration project I would want something so that initial cost + parts + materials + tools + my time =&gt; something worth more than I put into it, not something less.<P>  maybe Im taking your 'restore' too literally - if you want to get a cheap fiero and fix it up so you can drive it around and have fun with it - thats great - thats kinda what Im doing with mine - but if your thinking of a full blown restoration, then if it were me, i would get something like a 1900s car, an old roadster, an early Harley Davison or an Indian, something that was a milestone in automotive accomplishment or was a special/unique machine.<P>  Its getting harder and harder to find ways to spend your time on projects that dont duplicate stuff thats already out there - hang around ebay or autotrader.com for a while and you can find a nice 88 formula for about $3,000 - really low milage ones for $6,000 or so.<P> You are still young and you feel like you have all the time in the world - dont sell yourself short - aim high - fieros may be uncommon where you live but they were a production car and 100,000s of them were produced. When you get done restoring a car you will be an expert on it - why not be an expert on something really special or unique?||||reg||1
Z||000015||p4n1c||08-22-2001||04:32 PM||||I would like a '69 Hurst/Olds, but I can't afford it or the gas needed for the Rocket 455.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>It won't be a full blown, hardcore restore.  It'll be mostly a big take it apart, clean, rebuild on existing part type job.  Just to clean it up, make it look clean and exotic.<P>I do sometime in my life want to do a full blown restore on a '69 Hurst/Olds like I said.  My uncle has a early-mid 60's 4-4-2 that just blows my mind of how good of condition it's in and how fast that damn thing goes.<P>I need a replacement for my Cutlass as it's getting up past 410,000kms and is starting to give little problems that are costing as much as I could be into performance, etc.<P>I do think your advice is right on.  If you want to full blown restore a car, engine and all.  It should be something vintage and something rare.  There are some Porsche's I would like to get too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  But the insurance for a 20 year old in Ontario is killer for cars like that.||||reg||1
